# Prime7 orchestration (balanced, cost-aware)

Details: `docs/ai-development/ORCHESTRATION.md` (workflow and gates), `docs/ai-development/MODEL-USAGE-POLICY.md` (models).
Agents live in `.cursor/agents/`.

**Priorities, in order:** correctness and data integrity, security and tenant isolation, business-workflow accuracy,
maintainability, tests, then cost. Quality rules (tenant checks, tests, independent review of risky work) still apply;
what changes is **who** does the work: Auto first, Premium only when it is really needed.

**This rule is an instruction, not platform enforcement.** Cursor enforces only the agent-file `model` and `readonly`
fields, its approval prompts and your dashboard spending limits.

## Cost rules (apply to every task)

1. **Do the work in the parent chat.** Subagents start from zero and re-read files, so each one costs extra. Launch one
   only when it adds clear value (independent review, or a large search that would flood this chat).
2. **Premium agents (`*-premium`) are opt-in.** Use one only when the user asks for it in this task ("use premium",
   names the agent, or "Treat this as CRITICAL with premium review"), or under rule 3. Otherwise, if you think Premium is
   justified, ask first with `AskQuestion` (name the agent and the one-line reason). Never start Premium silently.
3. **One automatic Premium step:** for CRITICAL work only, run `security-auditor-premium` once on the final diff.
   Everything else in CRITICAL work uses Auto agents unless the user approves more.
4. **Budget per task without asking:** at most **3 subagent runs** in total, at most **1 Premium run**, and at most
   **2 correction rounds** per failed gate. Beyond that, report to the user and ask.
5. **Keep context small:** prefer `Grep` and partial `Read` over reading whole large files; use `explore` with
   "quick" thoroughness; do not re-read files already in this chat; give subagents short, focused prompts.
6. Run tests yourself with the Shell tool (Docker / npm). Use `test-runner` only when the run is long or noisy.
7. Never use Max mode or Fast models on your own. Do not recommend pinning a premium model for the main chat.

## Modes (classify at the start; say the mode in one line; if unsure, pick the higher)

- **STANDARD (default):** routine and moderately complex work. The parent explores, implements and runs tests.
  Substantial changes get one independent Auto review (`qa-engineer`); security-sensitive ones get `security-auditor`.
- **ADVANCED:** new modules, architecture changes, complex integrations, major UI redesigns. The parent (or Auto
  `architect`) writes the plan, Auto `plan-auditor` gives the verdict, the parent or Auto developers implement, the
  parent runs tests, Auto `qa-engineer` reviews. Premium only with the user's OK.
- **CRITICAL:** finance, accounting, inventory valuation, posting, tenant isolation, authorization, migrations. Plan in
  writing with evidence-backed acceptance criteria and regression tests, Auto `plan-auditor` verdict, implement, run the
  focused plus neighbouring tests, then `security-auditor-premium` (the one automatic Premium step). Block completion
  while critical defects remain.

## Gates and limits

- Never let the implementer be the only reviewer of substantial or risky work. Code generation is not proof.
- A test passed only if executed output is visible in this chat.
- Read-only agents cannot run shell; pass them `git status`, `git diff` and test output in the prompt.
- Run at most **two** implementation agents at once, and only in separate Git worktrees with disjoint files.
- Ask the user before destructive actions, production migrations or deploys, force pushes, or production data changes.
- Never enable on-demand billing, raise spending limits or buy credits. If a Premium call fails, do not work around it;
  for CRITICAL work, stop and report.
- Name every Premium agent used (and why) in the final report. Never claim a subagent ran without a Task result.
